`git reset` moves the current branch pointer (`HEAD`) to a different commit and optionally changes the staging area and working directory.

## Common usages

### 1. Unstage files (keep your changes)

```bash
git reset <file>
```

Example:

```bash
git reset app.js
```

Equivalent modern command:

```bash
git restore --staged app.js
```

---

### 2. Undo last commit but keep changes staged

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

* Removes the last commit
* Keeps all changes staged

---

### 3. Undo last commit and unstage changes

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

(`--mixed` is the default)

* Removes the last commit
* Keeps changes in your files
* Unstages them

---

### 4. Completely discard last commit and changes

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

⚠️ Dangerous:

* Deletes commits and local changes permanently (unless recoverable via reflog)

---

### 5. Reset to a specific commit

```bash
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
```

Example:

```bash
git reset --hard a1b2c3d
```

---

## Difference between modes

| Mode      | Commit History | Staging Area | Working Files  |
| --------- | -------------- | ------------ | -------------- |
| `--soft`  | Reset          | Keep         | Keep           |
| `--mixed` | Reset          | Clear        | Keep           |
| `--hard`  | Reset          | Clear        | Delete changes |

---

## Recovering after a bad reset

Use:

```bash
git reflog
```

Then reset back:

```bash
git reset --hard <reflog-commit>
```

