## `git reset` vs `git revert`

### `git reset`

Rewrites history by moving the branch pointer.

Example:

```bash id="r1"
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

Before:

```text
A -- B -- C (HEAD)
```

After:

```text
A -- B (HEAD)
```

Commit `C` disappears from branch history.

Best for:

* Local commits
* Cleaning up before pushing

Avoid on shared branches unless you know what you're doing.

---

### `git revert`

Creates a new commit that undoes changes.

Example:

```bash id="r2"
git revert HEAD
```

Before:

```text
A -- B -- C (HEAD)
```

After:

```text
A -- B -- C -- D (HEAD)
             ^
             D reverses C
```

Best for:

* Shared branches
* Safe undo after pushing

---

# Quick Rule

| Situation                 | Use          |
| ------------------------- | ------------ |
| Undo local commit         | `git reset`  |
| Undo pushed/shared commit | `git revert` |
| Keep history clean        | `git reset`  |
| Preserve history          | `git revert` |

---

# Useful Real Examples

## Undo last commit but keep files

```bash id="r3"
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

You can recommit differently.

---

## Remove accidental files from commit

```bash id="r4"
git reset HEAD file.txt
```

Then recommit.

---

## Undo pushed commit safely

```bash id="r5"
git revert HEAD
git push
```

---

## Reset branch to remote state

```bash id="r6"
git fetch origin
git reset --hard origin/main
```

Deletes local changes and matches remote exactly.

---

# Visual Mental Model

Git has 3 areas:

```text
Working Directory  ->  Staging Area  ->  Repository
(files)               (index)           (commits)
```

`git reset` can affect:

* commits
* staging
* files

depending on mode (`soft`, `mixed`, `hard`).
